        "timestamp": 1780992565
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Now I see that when I hover over it, there's the same message and also in the top left corner there is a <code>ℝ</code> sign.</p>",
        "id": 601355137,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Novak",
        "timestamp": 1780992735
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Here is how it looks like to me on <a href=\"http://live.lean-lang.org\">live.lean-lang.org</a> : <br>\n<img alt=\"image.png\" class=\"inline-image\" data-original-content-type=\"image/png\" data-original-dimensions=\"549x219\" data-original-src=\"/user_uploads/3121/VUN_Twptrhxkga95h_wlewU3/image.png\" src=\"/user_uploads/thumbnail/3121/VUN_Twptrhxkga95h_wlewU3/image.png/840x560.webp\"></p>",
        "id": 601357072,
        "sender_full_name": "Yaël Dillies",
        "timestamp": 1780993194
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oh, when I do the same, but over the upwords arrow in the code itself I get ℝ, but when I do it like you over the arrow in the infoview I get what you get. Why are they different? Could you explain what the function in the picture you sent is doing?</p>",
        "id": 601359138,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Novak",
        "timestamp": 1780993694
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It's a bit embarrassing, but turns out I can just do this:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">mwe</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">EuclideanSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Fin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">)}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">‖‖</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"bp\">‖⁻¹</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"bp\">‖</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">norm_smul_inv_norm</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">𝕜</span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>and there's no problem, and also in the real theorem I'm working on, there's something similar that works. Although I still don't fully understand what's going on here and why the rewrite didn't work. But practically the problem is solved.</p>",
        "id": 601361310,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Novak",
        "timestamp": 1780994223
    },
    {
        "content": "<blockquote>\n<p>Although I still don't fully understand what's going on here and why the rewrite didn't work.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>One clue: when you look at the infoview in your example, the type of <code>h2</code> is <code>‖x‖⁻¹ = ‖x‖⁻¹</code>. That isn't a very helpful proposition!</p>\n<p>(The issue here is that you wrote <code>↑</code> but you didn't specify which coercion you were talking about.)</p>",
        "id": 601950302,
        "sender_full_name": "Matt Diamond",
        "timestamp": 1781132843
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Well you have no way to have that coercion automatically inserted, it's a coercion from <code>ℝ</code> to <code>ℝ</code> called <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=RCLike.ofReal#doc\">docs#RCLike.ofReal</a>. To give you an understanding of why there is a coercion: The lemma also works for complex numbers, in which case you need the coercion <code>ℝ → ℂ</code> here. In order to be general for both <code>ℝ</code> and <code>ℂ</code>, the lemma used <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=RCLike#doc\">docs#RCLike</a> and its <code>ℝ → X</code> coercion <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=RCLike.ofReal#doc\">docs#RCLike.ofReal</a>, which turns out to be trivial for <code>X := ℝ</code>. Also the lemma you were looking for to get rid of that coercion was <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=RCLike.ofReal_real_eq_id#doc\">docs#RCLike.ofReal_real_eq_id</a>, although <code>simp at h1</code> takes a bit of a more funny path through the uniqueness of a ring homomorphism from <code>ℝ</code> to <code>ℝ</code>, i.e. <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Real.ringHom_apply#doc\">docs#Real.ringHom_apply</a>.</p>",
        "id": 602304695,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1781214345
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thank you both for the explanations!</p>",
        "id": 602391578,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Novak",
        "timestamp": 1781241920
    }
